From urbanek at research.att.com  Mon Sep 11 13:48:23 2006
From: urbanek at research.att.com (Simon Urbanek)
Date: Mon, 11 Sep 2006 09:48:23 -0400
Subject: Re: Correctly assessing the result of a library() call through REngine
In-Reply-To: <1157973124.27703.3.camel@localhost>
References: <1157973124.27703.3.camel@localhost>
Message-ID: <C844B311-08A4-4815-9DCC-14E6B258E08C@research.att.com>

Adrian,

On Sep 11, 2006, at 7:12 AM, Adrian Custer wrote:

> Hey all,
>
> After initializing an REngine, I call re.eval("library(sp)"); to  
> load that library. For now, I simply assume the call works but I  
> wonder if you all don't have a standard code block to assess the  
> result of this call and catch the possibility that the call failed.
>
> Any suggestions or examples?
>

I have just checked and was horrified that I omitted boolean return  
type conversion. Therefore for now you can use something like this:

if (re.eval("as.integer(library(foo,logical.return=TRUE))").asInt() 
==1) {
  System.out.println("package was loaded successfully.");
...
}

i'll add boolean type conversion asap, then you'll be able to use
if (re.eval("library(foo,logical.return=TRUE))").asBool().isTRUE())  
{ .. }
but in JRI 0.3-1 it will fail, because eval returns null instead of  
the corresponding REXP.

Cheers,
Simon



